Next:
 - Naming convention. Constraints - records can be up to 60 characters. 
   - Not sure what full component name of VCU1000 will be - prob will need to 
     change this later to comply with what is actually assigned. 
   - Convention for getter/setter pairs seems to be that the getter record is 
     the variable name, while the setter is the getter name with ":SET" appended.
     e.g.
       $(device):P
       $(device):P:SET
     Do we prefer 
       - :subsys:<param>:<action> (e.g. V:MAX:SET)
       - :subsys:[<verb>]<param> (e.g. V:SETMAX)?
     Nothing in convention to prevent 1st, but all examples I've seen seem to 
     prefer the second, so will adopt this.
     
 - Determine process flow - which records will scan periodically, and which will
   be passive? For the former, should some records process more/ less frequently?
   For example, for fields that we don't expect to change (e.g. version), could
   we process once at startup and then never again afterwards?
-  Note pattern used in GP app (and elsewhere) - single heartbeat fanout record 
   linked to all input records. Can then disable fanout to disable comms. As 
   input records passive, we can also link to them from other places if we want 
   to process without waiting for next periodic scan. 
   
-  Consider structure - does it make sense to decompose into more than one template?
   Alternative is we could group records by subcomponent in vdb file. 
   
-  Consider internal logic of database - besides the basic setter/getters and the 
   heartbeat, what other functions should the database perform? Do we want to 
   have some internal interlocks to disable some records when they are not valid?
   What records will we need to support this?
-  One option is to intentionally perform no error checking - this will mean we 
   are not suppressing log messages, and hopefully will result in the record 
   alarm status being set to Invalid. 
-  Need manual voltage setting to work sensibly. 2 issues to consider:
    - Setting voltage will do nothing (but still appear to work) if Regulation is
      Off. 
    - We might want to provide some feedback to user that pressure is within 
      prescribed tolerance in supervisory mode. In particular may be a bit odd
      if the Status field reads "Standby" - could have a derived field such as
      "Pressure correct" which operates independently of the status field. 
-  Might want to implement our own regulation timeout countdown in the database,
   since the device doesn't report this directly.
-  Interlocks we would require:
    - Remote mode -> Every SET message other than RemoteMode
    - Timer + Regulation -> SET TimerTarget
    - Regulation -> SET ValueKI, ValueKP, ValveVoltage
-  Handling of out-of-range values - at present these generate an error message from
   device. Is this sensible? Will result in the setter record being marked Invalid.
   - Found solution to this problem - use the DRVH and DRVL fields of ao (and 
   presumably longout). These will truncate out-of-range values for VAL to these
   limits before writing to OVAL, so we should be able to effectively eliminate 
   out-of-range errors this way. 
-  May want a few records which rely on status/regulation value but don't report
   directly - 
    - A reset function for the Error state which send "SET Regulation Off".
    - A toggle between "closed loop" and "supervisory" which controls whether 
      you can input voltage directly, independent of actually engaging regulation.
      
      
      
I/O readback:
 - would be nice if we could set up some means to have a text control in EDM 
   display the RBV, not the setpoint - perhaps could have an FLINK and INLINK 
   from the input record to the output, so that if for some reason the write fails
   the GUI will reflect this. Trouble is I think we may have a race condition 
   then - input could overwrite output with old value before new value has 
   propagated through to input, and we end up writing old value back to device. 
   Use an NPP link? Then output record should show right value for readback to 
   GUI, but won't actually write to device (and then new value should propagate
   back to the field once device reports it has changed). 
